So Production has at least two separate dimensions:

> **Creative commitment** = how settled/chosen the creative proposition and its elements are.  
> **Production lifecycle** = where the filmmaking undertaking is operationally.

That prevents nonsense like using one `status` field to mean both “we chose this creative direction” and “the Production is currently finishing.”

## Checkpoint at 250

This last stretch has also clarified something important about **Production itself**. It is now clearly more than execution:

- it can begin early enough for Format/Distribution Strategy to influence Story development;
- it carries Production Purpose and Success Criteria;
- it can exist as an exploratory/candidate Production before authorization;
- candidate Productions can be forecast and strategically compared without committing spend;
- prioritization/deferral decisions preserve their assumptions;
- deferred Productions don't silently drift with evolving Universe/Story state;
- reconsideration uses normal version/branch semantics;
- and now its operational lifecycle is explicitly separate from creative commitment.

That substantially strengthens our decision to **reject Project**. Production already is the bounded filmmaking undertaking we needed.

